There are several ways to center a `div` horizontally in CSS. Here are the most common and effective methods:

## 1. **Using `margin: auto` (Most Common)**

```css
.centered-div {
  width: 500px; /* Must have a width */
  margin: 0 auto;
}
```

This works when the element has a defined width and is a block-level element.

## 2. **Using Flexbox (Recommended for Modern Projects)**

```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
}

.centered-div {
  /* No need for width or margin */
}
```

## 3. **Using CSS Grid (Modern Alternative)**

```css
.container {
  display: grid;
  justify-items: center;
}

.centered-div {
  /* No need for width or margin */
}
```

## 4. **Using `text-align` (For inline/inline-block elements)**

```css
.container {
  text-align: center;
}

.centered-div {
  display: inline-block;
}
```

## 5. **Using `position` and `transform` (Precise control)**

```css
.centered-div {
  position: absolute;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translateX(-50%);
  width: 500px; /* Required for proper positioning */
}
```

## **Best Practice Recommendation**

For modern websites, **Flexbox** (method #2) is generally the best choice because:
- It's semantic and intuitive
- Works well with responsive design
- Doesn't require fixed widths in many cases
- Has excellent browser support

```css
/* Example with Flexbox */
.container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center; /* Optional: center vertically too */
  height: 100vh; /* Full viewport height */
}

.centered-div {
  width: 300px;
  height: 200px;
  background: #f0f0f0;
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
}
```

Choose the method that best fits your layout needs!
